<section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow"><span></span>AAB insights</p><h1>Ideas for science that remembers how it learned.</h1><p>Short perspectives on evidence, uncertainty, failure, governance and the infrastructure required to preserve scientific understanding.</p></section>
<section class="launch-section"><div class="launch-heading"><p class="eyebrow dark"><span></span>Foundation series</p><h2>The questions behind AAB.</h2><p>This initial series establishes AAB’s public intellectual foundation. Full articles can be added progressively as research, pilots and institutional dialogue create evidence worth publishing.</p></div><div class="insight-grid"><article class="insight-card"><span>SCIENTIFIC MEMORY</span><h3>Why conclusions are not enough</h3><p>A conclusion without its evidence, uncertainty and reasoning becomes difficult to challenge—and easy to misuse.</p><strong>Foundation perspective</strong></article><article class="insight-card"><span>NEGATIVE LEARNING</span><h3>Why failure belongs in scientific memory</h3><p>Failed work can prevent repetition, expose conditions and reveal mechanisms that a successful result never shows.</p><strong>Foundation perspective</strong></article><article class="insight-card"><span>GOVERNED INTELLIGENCE</span><h3>Why powerful systems need visible boundaries</h3><p>Intelligence becomes more trustworthy when facts, scenarios, recommendations and authority remain explicitly separate.</p><strong>Foundation perspective</strong></article><article class="insight-card"><span>UNCERTAINTY</span><h3>Unknown is a scientific state</h3><p>Missing evidence should create a named knowledge gap—not a confident-looking answer generated from absence.</p><strong>Foundation perspective</strong></article><article class="insight-card"><span>AGRICULTURE</span><h3>The first proving domain</h3><p>Agriculture connects formulations, environments, trials, field observations and outcomes in a demanding real-world learning chain.</p><strong>Foundation perspective</strong></article><article class="insight-card"><span>CROSS-DOMAIN FUTURE</span><h3>From agriculture to shared scientific infrastructure</h3><p>The same governed pattern can support future water, environment and research domains while preserving their distinct evidence rules.</p><strong>Future direction</strong></article></div></section>
